import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/JsBenchResults
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark: what the benchmark is, the results beside the frameworks it
## is compared with (`JsBenchResults`, from site/js-framework-benchmark.json, which `just js-bench
## bench` measures), what they say, the entries, and how to run it (benchmarks/jsframework/).
meta = {
	title: "js-framework-benchmark · Examples",
	description: "Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark beside vanillajs, Solid, Leptos, Joy and React: CPU time, memory, download and first paint, what the numbers say, and how to run it.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "glance", label: "At a glance" },
		{ id: "versus", label: "Roc Solid against each" },
		{ id: "charts", label: "Each category" },
		{ id: "numbers", label: "All the numbers" },
		{ id: "reading", label: "What they say" },
		{ id: "entries", label: "The entries" },
		{ id: "run", label: "Run it" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<NoHydration>
		<PageHero
			eyebrow="Examples · Benchmarks"
			title="Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark"
		>
			js-framework-benchmark is the yardstick most web frameworks are measured with: one page, a
			table of rows and six buttons. Its runner opens each framework's page in Chrome, clicks, and
			times until the page has painted; it also measures memory and downloads. Roc Solid's two entries
			are in <code>benchmarks/jsframework/</code>, here beside vanillajs (the floor: hand-written DOM
			code), Solid (the closest relative), Leptos (signals in Rust and wasm), Joy (the other Roc
			framework) and React.
		</PageHero>

		<JsBenchResults />

		<Heading id="reading">What they say</Heading>
		<p>
			The first paint is the best of them: the page is server-rendered HTML (here, files written by
			<code>rocx build --static</code>), shown before its wasm loads. Ready to use is the reverse: the
			page answers a click only once the runtime and the wasm have arrived and hydrated it. On the
			benchmark's server, which compresses each file slowly as it sends it, that is the last of them.
			The download is most of that: the runtime ships only what the page uses (D-087), 128 KB of its 180
			in the run shown, but with the wasm it is still more than Joy's, Leptos's or React's, and many
			times Solid's, which is JavaScript alone.
		</p>
		<p>
			Updating existing rows is close to the others: a row's holes rerun only when its data changed
			(compared with <code>==</code>), selecting one runs the two rows it leaves and comes to
			(<code>create_selector!</code>, D-083), and a swap moves the two rows that moved. Making rows is
			slower: about twice vanillajs, for 1,000 rows and for 10,000. A row is a template (D-084): its
			markup comes once, and each row is a copy with its parts put in, so Roc builds no markup beyond its
			holes. But each hole is its own computation, run by a call into the wasm, noting what it read for
			the next change; vanillajs writes the text and is done. Memory follows: a computation for the row
			and each hole, each with what it read. The repository's <code>TODO.md</code> lists what is left.
		</p>
		<p>How CPU time got here, against the fastest:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>5×</strong> in the first run (D-074). <strong>2.06×</strong> after fixing how the host noted
				what runs allocate, and how a <code>.map</code> reorders rows.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>1.83×</strong> (React: 1.74×) after D-083 to D-087, on 2026-11-25 and 26: the selector,
				templates, cheaper notes, fewer calls per row, the runtime shaken to what a page uses. Creating
				1,000 rows: 45.8 → 39.3 ms; selecting one: 11.8 → 7.7; download: 73.4 → 66.2 KB. Each step was
				measured alone. Two were taken back for their cost elsewhere: preload hints (a later first paint)
				and delegated handlers (more memory).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>1.68×</strong> (React: 1.70×) in the run shown (2026-11-26, at 505b0e7), after the
				browser's host became Rust (D-090, its memory shared with the phone's, D-091), a list whose rows
				all go is emptied at once, and each computation keeps less. Creating 1,000 rows: 39.3 → 36.1 ms;
				10,000: 448 → 381; replacing 1,000: 43.3 → 40.5; appending: 46.6 → 42.1; selecting one: 7.7 →
				6.2; clearing: 19.8 → 15.0; run memory: 4.8 → 4.5 MB; first paint: 37 → 30 ms; ready to use: 288
				→ 262 ms. The download grew from 66.2 to 67.7 KB with the runtime features that landed meanwhile.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<Heading id="entries">The entries</Heading>
		<p>
			Each entry is a one-page app with the vanillajs entry's markup (the runner finds buttons by id and
			rows by class). <code>keyed</code> keeps each row by its id (<code>.map</code> with
			<code>{"key={row.id}"}</code>), so a row moves with its data; <code>non-keyed</code> keeps a row by
			position (<code>.map_with_index</code> with <code>{"key={i}"}</code>), so a swap rewrites the two
			rows instead. The rows' data is plain Roc (<code>Rows.roc</code>): an id and a three-word label
			from a fixed-seed generator. Nothing is batched or cached beyond what an app would do, and the
			wasm is built as an app ships it.
		</p>
		<p>
			The others are the benchmark's own entries, built as its README says (Leptos's build is in its
			repository). Joy's entries aren't in the benchmark yet; they're built from its 0.35.0 release as
			Joy's <code>benchmarks/jsframework/README.md</code> says. Upstream retired its non-keyed entries
			in October 2026, but its runner still measures them, so ours, Joy's and vanillajs's are kept.
		</p>

		<Heading id="run">Run it</Heading>
		<p>
			The benchmark lives outside the repository, in a clone of krausest/js-framework-benchmark with its
			runner installed, the other entries built and a Chrome for the runner; set it up once, as
			<code>benchmarks/jsframework/README.md</code> says. The runner needs a small patch (in the same
			folder): it counted events from before tracing began as the click's. Then:
		</p>
		<Code source={"just js-bench entries   # Roc Solid's two entries, built into the clone\njust js-bench bench     # them, then two rounds of every entry (about 75 minutes)\njust js-bench bench --rounds=1 --count=3 --benchmarks=01_,05_   # a quick look"} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
		<p>
			The runner is headless, the benchmark's server on port 8089; each round runs one benchmark at a time
			for every entry, the second round in reverse order. Run it on mains, with nothing else heavy
			running. Results land in <code>site/js-framework-benchmark.json</code>, which this page reads. Like
			the <a href={Paths.examples}>examples</a>' numbers, they're measured only when asked, never by the
			site's build.
		</p>
	</NoHydration>
